To reproduce the results, we strongly suggest to Docker.
We provide a Dockerfile to build a docker image that contains
everything you need. Assuming you have docker installed on your machine,
you can run the following command in the "code/" directory
to build the docker image:

    docker build -t eurocrypt_2027 .

You can then run the container as follows:

    # Make sure to run from the "code/" directory.
    docker run -p 8888:8888 -v $(pwd):/home/sage/mnt -it eurocrypt_2027

To reproduce the numerical experiments of the dual attack, run the following
commands *inside the container*:

    # run sage:
    sage
    # then enter the following commands *inside sage*
    import sys
    sys.path.append('/home/sage/lattice-estimator')
    attach("estimator_eurocrypt.py")
    # run the optimizer (takes about 2 hours)
    %time results = runall()
    %time results2 = runall(red_cost_model = QuantumABFKSW20())
    %time results3 = runall(red_cost_model = BCSS23())
    # optional: produce the latex table of the article
    print(results_table_latex(results))
    print(results_table_latex(results2))
    print(results_table_latex(results3))
    # or just reproduce the results of the paper.
    reproduce_paper(EUROCRYPT_PARAMS_QUANTUM_MATZOV)
    reproduce_paper(EUROCRYPT_PARAMS_QUANTUM_QABFKSW20)
    reproduce_paper(EUROCRYPT_PARAMS_QUANTUM_BCSS23)

Technical details: the docker image built does not include the code, instead
it expects the code to be available at the mount point /home/sage/mnt inside
the container. This allows you to edit the code outside of the container
while running the container. Furthermore, in order to run the jupyter notebook
from inside the container, you need to forward the port 8888 so that you can
use it from your browser.